WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
A virus waits on a light switch, then reaches a cell that makes new copies.

2
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.520
Was the virus alive on the switch?

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.000
Watch what each object can do, and who supplies the work.

4
00:00:15.433 --> 00:00:19.333
Biologists organize evidence with characteristics of life.

5
00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:25.373
Living things have cells, use energy, grow, respond, keep conditions steady,

6
00:00:25.713 --> 00:00:26.773
reproduce, and evolve.

7
00:00:28.153 --> 00:00:29.433
The list guides a decision.

8
00:00:30.693 --> 00:00:33.273
It is not a score where an individual tick wins.

9
00:00:34.417 --> 00:00:35.937
Bring in a bacterium.

10
00:00:36.997 --> 00:00:41.577
It is a single cell with a membrane, cytoplasm, genes, and ribosomes.

11
00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:47.817
This prokaryote has no nucleus and, typically, no membrane-covered organelles.

12
00:00:48.897 --> 00:00:52.077
In suitable conditions, materials cross its membrane.

13
00:00:53.457 --> 00:00:55.077
Its ribosomes build proteins.

14
00:00:56.377 --> 00:00:59.317
It uses energy, grows, responds, and divides.

15
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.010
Now bring back the virus.

16
00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:07.050
It carries genes inside a protein capsid, but it is not a cell.

17
00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.890
The capsid protects the genes.

18
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.310
Some viruses add a fatty envelope outside the capsid.

19
00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:17.890
It has no cytoplasm or ribosomes.

20
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.870
Outside a host, it does not take in food, use energy, or grow.

21
00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:27.513
Predict what changes when the virus enters the host cell.

22
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:32.833
Does the virus gain cell machinery, or does the host supply what it lacks?

23
00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.613
Keep your eye on the ribosomes.

24
00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:43.767
The host supplies what the virus lacks.

25
00:01:45.047 --> 00:01:48.127
It provides ribosomes, energy, and building blocks.

26
00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:51.867
Viral genes use them to make virus parts.

27
00:01:53.427 --> 00:01:58.107
Host enzymes or viral enzymes copy the viral genes, depending on the virus.

28
00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:01.247
The parts assemble around the copies.

29
00:02:02.447 --> 00:02:05.027
The virus never gains its own metabolism.

30
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:09.117
Edge cases show why a box cannot decide alone.

31
00:02:10.377 --> 00:02:14.117
A mature red blood cell cannot divide, yet it remains a cell.

32
00:02:15.357 --> 00:02:19.037
A crystal gets bigger, but it has no cells and no metabolism.

33
00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:21.917
Growth alone is not enough.

34
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:28.380
Viruses carry genes and evolve, so scientists still discuss their place near life's

35
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:35.460
edge. Virginia biology classes viruses as nonliving because they are not cells and

36
00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.340
have no metabolism of their own.

37
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.500
They depend on a host cell to be copied.

38
00:02:41.633 --> 00:02:42.993
Return to the switch.

39
00:02:44.293 --> 00:02:48.433
The virus can wait there and later be copied, but waiting is not living.

40
00:02:49.693 --> 00:02:52.413
The host cell supplies the ribosomes and energy.

41
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:58.413
The bacterium carries its own cell machinery and can act when conditions suit it.

42
00:02:59.317 --> 00:03:00.817
So ask these questions.

43
00:03:02.177 --> 00:03:05.617
Is it a cell, and does it have its own ribosomes and metabolism?

44
00:03:07.017 --> 00:03:08.417
That leads to the course rule.

45
00:03:09.777 --> 00:03:14.177
Bacteria are living cells; viruses are not cells and are classed nonliving.
